  "prediction": "**Wildlife Trafficking: Review of U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service and National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's Use of Financial Rewards**\n\nThe U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (FWS) and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) have laws that authorize the payment of financial rewards for information on wildlife trafficking. However, the agencies have not prioritized the use of rewards and have not reviewed their effectiveness in combating wildlife trafficking.\n\nFrom fiscal years 2007 to 2017, FWS reported paying 25 rewards for a total of $184,500, while NOAA reported paying 2 rewards for a total of $21,000. However, agency officials could not provide sufficient assurance that these numbers represented all rewards paid during this period.\n\nFWS and NOAA have policies outlining the process for preparing and submitting reward requests, but FWS's policy does not specify factors for agents to consider when developing proposed reward amounts. NOAA's policy, on the other hand, outlines factors such as the risk to the informant and the potential impact of the information provided.\n\nThe agencies have also communicated little information to the public on the potential availability of rewards, which may discourage people from coming forward with information on wildlife trafficking.\n\nTo improve their use of financial rewards, the GAO recommends that FWS and NOAA:\n\n1. Track financial reward information so that it is clearly documented and readily available for examination.\n2. Augment their financial reward policies to specify factors for agents to consider when developing proposed reward amounts.\n3. Determine the types of additional information to communicate to the public on rewards and develop a plan to do so.\n4. Review the effectiveness of their use of financial rewards and consider potential improvements.\n\nBy implementing these recommendations, FWS and NOAA can improve their chances of obtaining information on wildlife trafficking and better manage their use of financial rewards as a law enforcement tool.",
